A||||2||Ralfy9862||Audio help!||||1||
Z||000000||Ralfy9862||01-30-2001||06:52 AM||||I finally got my factory sub to work with my aftermarket deck with help from you guy but now i have another problem my left front speaker wont work. It is wired to a tan wire which is positive and then there are two grey wires to hook up the negative to when i hook up the speaker negative to the plain grey wire all speakers work but not sub. but if i hook the speaker up to the grey wire with a black line on it the sub works but not my left front speaker. is this beacasue the grey wire with the black line must be grounded to something. If i ground that wire will everything work right? ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Seanh||01-30-2001||08:58 AM||||Interesting. . . I will have to look at some wiring diagrams, Iv never heard, or seen this in a Fiero before, but then again I only have 2. Sounds strange tho... Did you ever try just hooking up both wires to the speaker negative? Unless someone else chimes in about this telling you its okay, I wouldnt ground that wire until I have a chance to look at some diagrams. Do try hooking them both up to the speaker negative tho and see what happens.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||01-30-2001||10:33 AM||||Left Rear = brown + yellow -<BR>Right rear = dk blue + light blue -<BR>left front = Gray with black stripe + Tan -<BR>right front = dk green + light green -<P>The dark colors are + the light are -<P>The radio wires are:<BR>Yellow  = acc power<BR>brown = dimmer control from Headlight switch<BR>Orange = constant power<BR>gray = panel lights<BR>black = ground<BR>pink (2 wires) = subwoofer<P>I wouldn't ground the gray with black stripe. It may short out the driver for that speaker. Most aftermarket stereo's are sensative to this if they have a 4 channel amp internal to them.<BR>Hope this helps.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-30-2001).]||||reg||1
